  {
   "id": "ciZ3_9cqBAAJ",
   "from": "Aki Vehtari",
   "author": "a236d3d356ca53fd",
   "date": "2015-11-07T19:11:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: normalize student_t by sqrt((nu-2)/nu) additonally MATT trick?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Andre,<div><br></div><div>Interesting question. Your re-parameterisation idea scales all variables equally, and the making the variances to match doesn&#39;t necessarily make the sampling more efficient as the tail behavior also changes, The next step would be to take into account also the change in the tail shape by using a transformation which maps the values from normal(0,1) to student_t(nu,mu,sigma).</div><div><br></div><div>Aki</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Saturday, November 7, 2015 at 2:48:44 AM UTC+2, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">andre.p...@googlemail.com</a> wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi everybody,<div><br></div><div>in STAN is it wise to use:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> student_t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sqrt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">((</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)/</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">));</span></div></code></div><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(102,102,0);background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><br></span></div><div> instead of:<br></div><div><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(102,102,0);background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><br></span></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> student_t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><font color=\"#006666\"><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>maybe we should additionally MATT trick?</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sqrt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">((</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)/</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> student_t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>Andre</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "JQhANAxTBAAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-11-08T07:27:50Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] normalize student_t by sqrt((nu-2)/nu) additonally MATT trick?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Aside from the geometric considerations of parameterization,\r<br>make sure you have a constraint\r<br>\r<br>  real&lt;lower=2&gt; nu;\r<br>\r<br>because the rescaling assumes n &gt;= 2.\r<br>\r<br>And you have to be careful to convert sigma back to the original\r<br>scale if you need to make external comparisons.\r<br>\r<br>We&#39;re going to have to start a parameterization page to go\r<br>along with our priors page.  We could eventually start building\r<br>in some of these parameterizations directly.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Nov 7, 2015, at 2:11 PM, Aki Vehtari &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">aki.v...@aalto.fi</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hi Andre,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Interesting question. Your re-parameterisation idea scales all variables equally, and the making the variances to match doesn&#39;t necessarily make the sampling more efficient as the tail behavior also changes, The next step would be to take into account also the change in the tail shape by using a transformation which maps the values from normal(0,1) to student_t(nu,mu,sigma).\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Aki\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; On Saturday, November 7, 2015 at 2:48:44 AM UTC+2, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">andre.p...@googlemail.com</a> wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi everybody,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; in STAN is it wise to use:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   y ~ student_t(nu,mu,sigma*sqrt((nu-2)/nu));\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  instead of:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   y ~ student_t(nu,mu,sigma);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; maybe we should additionally MATT trick?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; (y-mu)*sigma*sqrt((nu-2)/nu) ~ student_t(nu,0,1.0);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Andre\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "&gt; -- \r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;Stan users mailing list&quot; group.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; To post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463222324000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2mZ4QAlOBzBWZlUI4wGPrt\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B-36Cf9hBAAJ",
   "from": "andre.p...@googlemail.com",
   "author": "71c315ee557ff234",
   "date": "2015-11-08T12:01:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: normalize student_t by sqrt((nu-2)/nu) additonally MATT trick?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you Andrew, Aki, Bob, (in order written back)<div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span></font><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">  nu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2.0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">  err </span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> student_t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sqrt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">((</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)/</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">));</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// inverse multi. by student-t&#39;s nu/(nu-2)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>I get excellent results with above. I prescale my data to unit variance (var=1)  before processing in STAN.<br></div></div><div>When I don&#39;t, put in the correction factor above, the results are not as good. I assume that it has to do, that</div><div>sigma become a function of nu otherwise.</div><div><br></div><div><div>Andrew,</div><div>fixed nu in student-t is save.</div><div><br></div><div>Aki,</div><div>&quot;map values from normal(0,1) to student_t(nu,mu,sigma)&quot;<br></div><div>That&#39;s an interesting idea. I see it the other way around however,</div><div>have transfer function and map the data from student_t(nu,0,1)</div><div>to normal(0,1), if its possible. </div><div>Maybe copula(s). I did it once STAN and it performed quite well. </div><div>(Have to put my head in math...)</div><div><br></div><div>Bob,</div><div>absolutely, we have to assure a min. nu. </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Andre</div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
